+
+### The offline OIDC stub
+
+`oidc_stub.py` is a threaded stdlib HTTP server (no docker, no egress) that
+implements the three things Lakekeeper's OIDC authentication (limes
+`JWKSWebAuthenticator`) needs: `/.well-known/openid-configuration`
+(`issuer` + `jwks_uri`, fetched once at `serve` startup), `/jwks` (RS256
+public key, generated at stub startup, matched by `kid`), and `/token`
+(`client_credentials` grant → RS256 JWT with `iss`/`sub=<client_id>`/`aud`/
+`exp=now+1h`). run.sh backgrounds it, health-checks the discovery endpoint,
+and points the server at it:
+
+```
+LAKEKEEPER__OPENID_PROVIDER_URI=http://localhost:8899   # idp id "oidc"
+LAKEKEEPER__OPENID_AUDIENCE=lakekeeper
+```
+
+Subjects map to Lakekeeper user ids as `oidc~<sub>` (subject-claim preference
+`oid` then `sub`; the stub only sets `sub`). run.sh mints two machine users
+and bootstraps:
+
+* `admin` → `oidc~admin`: run.sh POSTs `/management/v1/bootstrap` with this
+  token — the **first authenticated caller becomes the global admin** — and
+  the pytest fixtures then create the `uc_e2e` warehouse and all UC objects
+  as admin (creator == OpenFGA owner). Exported as `UC_TEST_TOKEN`.
+* `user2` → `oidc~user2`: a second principal with **no grants**; the tests
+  self-provision it (`POST /management/v1/user`, id extracted from the JWT)
+  and grant it privileges piecemeal. Exported as `UC_TEST_TOKEN_USER2`.
+
+Tests can also mint ad-hoc tokens from a running stub via
+`from oidc_stub import mint_token; mint_token("alice")`.
+
+### Authz matrix (`test_authz.py`, MODE=openfga)
+
+| # | check | expected |
+|---|---|---|
+| 1 | `/management/v1/info` | `authz-backend=openfga` (proves `migrate` wrote the fork's FGA model incl. volume/function/model types) |
+| 2 | anonymous / garbage-token UC request, anonymous bootstrap | `401` (authn middleware fail-closed) |
+| 3 | user2 `POST /management/v1/user {"update-if-exists": true}` | `200/201`, id `oidc~user2`; `whoami` resolves (create-on-first-login; users are NOT auto-registered by arbitrary calls) |
+| 4 | admin `GET .../volume/{id}/authorizer-actions` | exactly `read_assignments, grant_* (pass_grants/manage_grants/describe/select/modify), change_ownership` — creator/owner holds the full grant set |
+| 5 | user2 `GET /volumes/{full_name}` and READ_VOLUME temp-creds pre-grant | `404 NOT_FOUND` UC envelope (existence masking) |
+| 6 | admin `POST .../volume/{id}/assignments` `{"writes": [{"type": "describe", "user": "oidc~user2"}, {"type": "select", "user": "oidc~user2"}]}` | `204`; assignments listing echoes both entries |
+| 7 | user2 volume GET / READ_VOLUME creds post-grant | `200` (vended AWS creds present) |
+| 8 | user2 WRITE_VOLUME creds post-grant | **real** `403 PERMISSION_DENIED` envelope (visible but `select` ≠ `modify`); own authorizer-actions stay empty (describe/select confer no grant powers) |
+| 9 | function: pre-grant `404` → grant `describe` → `200`; admin's authorizer-actions include `grant_execute` | function spot-check incl. execute grant action |
+| 10 | model: pre-grant `404` → grant `describe` → `200` | model spot-check |
+
+Volume `GET`/creds authorize directly on the volume object (`load_and_
+authorize_volume`), so a per-volume grant suffices — no namespace/warehouse
+grants are needed for visibility (the FGA model also inherits
+`describe/select/modify from parent` if you grant at namespace level
+instead).
 
